<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pyramid of the Sun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 200</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pyramid of the Sun is the largest building at Teotihuacan and one of the largest pyramids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, about 65 metres high, raised on the east side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avenue-of-the-dead">Avenue of the Dead</a> and probably completed by about 200 CE. A man-made cave runs beneath it. Its present form owes much to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopoldo-batres">Leopoldo Batres</a>'s reconstruction, completed in 1910.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyramid-of-the-sun.jpg" alt="Pyramid of the Sun" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Burkhard Mücke · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2020-02-11_Teotihuacan_la_Avenida_de_los_Muertos_y_la_Pir%C3%A1mide_del_Sol.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Pyramid of the Sun stands along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avenue-of-the-dead">Avenue of the Dead</a> at Teotihuacan, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-of-the-moon">Pyramid of the Moon</a> and the Ciudadela, in the shadow of the mountain Cerro Gordo. The name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>, who visited the city centuries after it was abandoned; the name the Teotihuacanos gave it is unknown. It was built in two phases. The first brought it nearly to its present size, and the second gave it its completed width of 225 metres and added an altar on top, which has not survived.</p><p>The structure was finished with lime plaster brought from surrounding areas and painted with brightly coloured murals. Neither paint nor plaster remains visible. Jaguar heads and paws, stars and snake rattles are among the few images associated with the pyramid. It is thought that the pyramid honoured a deity of Teotihuacan society, but little evidence supports this, and the destruction of the temple on top, by deliberate and natural forces, has prevented identification with any particular god.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The pyramid was built on a carefully chosen spot, from which it could be aligned with Cerro Gordo to the north and, at right angles, with sunrises and sunsets on specific dates. The central part of the city's grid, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avenue-of-the-dead">Avenue of the Dead</a>, repeats its orientation, while the southern part follows a slightly different one dictated by the Ciudadela.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>In 1959 the archaeologist Rene Millon and his team were among the first to study the tunnels beneath the pyramid. Most of the main tunnels had been sealed, though whether on purpose is open to interpretation. They yielded pottery, hearths and other artefacts, and Millon's team rejected the older theory that slaves built the pyramids, arguing that the craftsmanship suggests motivated builders.</p><p>In 1971 Ernesto Taboada found the entrance to a seven-metre pit at the foot of the main staircase, leading to a cave about six metres beneath the centre of the structure. It was first thought to be a natural lava tube and possibly Chicomoztoc, the place of origin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> legend. Later excavations suggest it is man-made and could have served as a royal tomb. Only a few caches of artefacts have been found, including obsidian arrowheads and human figurines, and children's burials lay at the corners of the pyramid, probably part of a rite dedicating its construction. The Teotihuacan Ocelot, found near its foot in the late nineteenth century, is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pyramid of the Sun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Pyramid of the Sun</b>, some 65 metres high, and early stages of the Pyramid of the Moon were raised in this period, and the city was laid out along the Avenue of the Dead on a grid oriented about 15.5 degrees east of north.</blockquote><a href="/empire/teotihuacan">Teotihuacan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…of Teotihuacan, near present-day Mexico City, dominated central Mexico from the 1st to the 6th centuries CE; its <b>Pyramid of the Sun</b>, completed around 200 CE, the Pyramid of the Moon and the Temple of the Feathered Serpent, at whose foundation some two hundred people were sacrificed, made…</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesoamerican-religion">Mesoamerican religion · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
